                    "value": "LABOUR CHANNEL: An estimated ~412,000 undocumented New Yorkers (NYC Mayor's Office of Immigrant Affairs, 2022; down from ~611,000 in 2012; NYC Comptroller estimated ~476,000 in 2019) are excluded from unemployment insurance and many labour protections, and are disproportionately exposed to wage theft. New York State's 2021 Excluded Workers Fund distributed more than $2B of its $2.1B appropriation to more than 130,000 approved applicants — 99% receiving the Tier 1 benefit of $15,600 — before exhausting by December 2021, with no permanent successor enacted as of mid-2026. CAPITAL CHANNEL: JPMorgan Chase (HQ: NYC) financed $58B in fossil fuels in 2025, up 12.6% and the largest of any bank in the world; Citigroup (HQ: NYC) is in the global top five at roughly $45-47B. Of the 2025 top five, only JPMorgan and Citigroup are NYC-headquartered — Bank of America ($47B) is Charlotte, MUFG ($47B) and Mizuho are Tokyo. The world's 65 largest banks financed fossil fuels with $906B in 2025 (+8% year-over-year) and $8.7 trillion since the 2016 Paris Agreement, with financing to fossil-fuel expanders up 27% to $508B. CIVIC COUNTERWEIGHT: three of NYC's five public pension systems (Teachers' Retirement System, NYCERS, Board of Education Retirement System) voted to divest from publicly-traded fossil-fuel reserve owners in 2021 and completed divestment in 2022, added an upstream private-markets exclusion in 2023, and hold a 2040 net-zero portfolio target with a $50B climate-solutions goal by 2035; weighted-average financed Scope 1+2 emissions fell 37% between 2019 and 2024, beating the 2025 interim targets a year early. Litigation challenging the divestment (Wong et al. v. NYCERS) was dismissed for lack of standing, affirmed on appeal, and the New York Court of Appeals denied leave to appeal on October 21, 2025 — final. Divestment has NOT been reversed.",
