                "globalBoroughs": null,
                "levelRationale": "Anchored on the headline: a 1.41% net rental vacancy rate against the 5% threshold in NY State's Emergency Tenant Protection Act, the statutory test for whether a 'housing emergency' exists (a LOWER rate means a WORSE emergency, and rent stabilization for ~996,600 units is sustained precisely because the market fails this test). At 3.5x below the line -- and 0.39% in the bottom rent quartile, meaning the affordable segment is functionally at zero -- the headline alone rules out anything at or below 'critical'. Adjusted UP by four material, well-sourced sub-indicators: (1) homelessness at an all-time record and still rising -- 194,531 unique individuals used DHS shelter during 2025, the most in system history, with ~96,500 people in shelter nightly in April 2026 and >350,000 New Yorkers without homes at 1 Jan 2026 -- reached AFTER the asylum-seeker population fell 51% from its Jan-2024 peak, so this is a structurally domestic record, not a migration shock; (2) the Right to Counsel collapse, from 71% of eligible tenants represented in Q4 FY2021 to 42% in Q4 FY2024 and 30% by March 2025, and 81% to 31% in the Bronx; (3) NYCHA's $78.3B twenty-year capital backlog, up 73% since 2017, with 633,808 households having applied for 200,000 Section 8 waitlist slots; (4) 86% of renter households earning under $25,000 outside means-tested housing severely rent burdened. Adjusted DOWN, and this is the reason the score is not 150: the citywide median rent-to-income ratio FELL from 34.3% to 29.5%, which NYCHVS calls the first decline since 2002, and NYCHVS's own conclusion is that 'the overall prevalence of rent burden in New York City declined for the first time in several survey cycles'; eviction filings fell 9.6% in 2024 and remain below pre-pandemic levels; and 2024-2026 delivered an unusually dense run of real instruments (City of Yes at 82,000 projected homes, Good Cause Eviction, 485-x, Local Law 126 of 2024, the 2026 rent freeze, the enacted pied-a-terre tax). Note also that the 4.54% 2021 comparator is described by NYCHVS itself as 'historically high', so the fall to 1.41% is reversion to and past NYC's chronic sub-3.5% norm rather than a new cliff. 135 therefore places housing as the most severe dimension in this portrait, clearly above 'critical', and just short of the scale ceiling -- with the measured improvement in median rent burden as the single named reason it is not 150.",
