                "levelRationale": "Anchored on the published headline: 20.6% of NYC workers drove alone to work (ACS 2024 1-Year, place-level), against the shared <50% threshold used in the Petaluma portrait. At 41% of the threshold this argues for roughly -85 on the headline alone. Moved up ~20 points under R1 for two material, independently verified structural failures inside the same dimension: (1) SUBWAY ACCESSIBILITY -- only 160 of 472 stations (33.9%) have stair-free access per MTA's own July 2026 count, leaving ~66% inaccessible, with the court-approved compliance horizon set at 2055; (2) BUS SPEEDS -- 8.17 mph citywide in 2024, DOWN 0.6% from 8.22 mph in 2015 and far short of the City's own 25%-improvement goal, with Manhattan at 6.29 mph. Also pulling up, though less: mean commute time of 40.6 minutes, among the longest of any large US city; Staten Island at 50.8% is the one borough above the threshold; Fair Fares enrolls only 41% of its ~940,000 eligible population; and NYC DOT has missed its Local Law 195 bus- and bike-lane mileage mandates every recent year. NOT moved further up, for three reasons independently verified in adversarial review: (a) TRAFFIC SAFETY IS A STRENGTH, NOT A SHORTFALL -- 2025 recorded 205 traffic deaths, the fewest since record-keeping began in 1910, down 19% year-on-year and 31% since Vision Zero launched in 2014, with Q1 2026 the third-lowest on record; an earlier version of this rationale asserted fatalities were at post-Vision-Zero highs, which primary NYC DOT sources refute; (b) the Fair Fares gap is smaller than previously stated (41% of ~940,000, not 37% of 1.4M) and is closing, with the income ceiling raised to 150% FPL and a further expansion to 200% FPL agreed in June 2026; (c) congestion pricing is operating, legally secure following Judge Liman's final ruling of 3 March 2026 in Metropolitan Transportation Authority v. Duffy, and delivering measured reductions (11% fewer vehicle entries, 27 million fewer entries in year one). Per R5, a strongly negative level is the correct and honest result for NYC mobility mode share and has not been nudged toward zero.",
