                "value": "~500 heat-related deaths per year citywide, May-September — about 3% of all warm-season deaths (2026 New York City Heat-Related Mortality Report, DOHMH, released 15 June 2026). This is a MULTI-YEAR ANNUAL AVERAGE, not a single-year count: an average of 7 direct heat-stress deaths/year (pooled 2016-2025) plus an average of ~490 modelled heat-exacerbated deaths/year (pooled 2014-2023), where heat aggravates an existing condition. EQUITY — SCOPE STATED PRECISELY: DOHMH's race-disaggregated rates cover DIRECT HEAT-STRESS DEATHS ONLY. For 2016-2025 the age-adjusted heat-stress death rate was 1.2 per million for Black New Yorkers and 1.0 for Latino New Yorkers against 0.4 for white New Yorkers — 3x and 2x respectively, in DOHMH's own words 'Black New Yorkers had heat-stress death rates three times higher and Latino New Yorkers had death rates two times higher than white New Yorkers.' DOHMH publishes NO race-disaggregated rate for the ~490/year heat-exacerbated component that makes up ~98% of the headline; for that component its inequity finding is qualitative ('inequitably affects Black New Yorkers, reflecting the impacts of structural racism'). The ratio and the headline therefore describe different populations and must not be conflated. Corroborating single-year fact: in 2025, 21 New Yorkers died of heat stress — the highest tally in over a decade, 19 of them during one four-day June heat wave — and 18 of the 21 were Black or Latino.",
                "year": 2026,
                "target": "No codified numeric mortality target exists, and no planetary boundary applies to this locally-defined dimension. The benchmark used for scoring is ZERO PREVENTABLE HEAT DEATHS plus elimination of the racial disparity in heat mortality — the aim DOHMH and Cool Neighborhoods NYC (2017) state in qualitative terms, pursued via AC access and affordability, cooling centres, tree canopy and cool roofs. Statutory commitments that exist (Local Law 41 of 2021 on resilient capital design; Local Law 128 of 2025's 7-cooling-centres-per-100,000 target) are progress measures reported in context and policyAnchors, never the scoring anchor.",
                "context": "In an 8.3M-person city, heat kills more people annually than all other weather hazards combined, and the mechanism is overwhelmingly indoor and socioeconomic (un-air-conditioned homes, and AC that households cannot afford to run) rather than purely meteorological — sharply different from Petaluma's wildfire-smoke-centered climate-health narrative. VINTAGE LADDER (R11), all confirmed from primary sources: the 2022 report (released 16 June 2022) gave ~370 deaths/year with race rates for 2011-2020 (Black 2.2 vs white 0.9 per million, 2x); the 2025 report (released 30 June 2025) gave ~525/year pooled 2018-2022, of which ~5/year heat-stress and ~520/year heat-exacerbated, with race rates for 2014-2023 (Black 0.8 vs white 0.4, 2x); the 2026 report gives ~500/year with race rates for 2016-2025 (Black 1.2, Latino 1.0, white 0.4, i.e. 3x and 2x). The step from ~370 to ~500-525 is driven principally by a moved pooling window and by DOHMH's successive refinements to the heat-exacerbated excess-mortality model — it is a methodology and scope shift, NOT a measured trend, and the portrait must not read it as a rise; note the current ~500 is in fact BELOW the previous vintage's ~525. The widening of the Black:white direct heat-stress ratio from 2x to 3x reflects the June 2025 heat wave entering the pooling window. The policy lever is AC access and its affordability (utility cost, not just ownership), tree canopy in redlined/EJ neighborhoods, and building-level cooling requirements; Local Law 97's building-emissions caps and heat-pump retrofit push interact with this because electrified cooling raises electricity demand even as it cuts direct heat-stress risk.",
