                "indicator": "NYC True Cost of Living (TCOL) Measure — share of residents below the true cost of living threshold",
                "value": "75.1% below the TCOL threshold — highest of the five boroughs and 13.3 points above citywide; 86.8% of persons in families with children; 15.6% below half the threshold (highest deep-hardship rate in the city); average resource gap $39,692. The report notes the Bronx has the lowest costs of any borough but also the least resources.",
                "year": 2022,
                "target": "The TCOL threshold is itself the benchmark: implied target 0% of residents below it. Charter Amendment 3 (November 2022, approved by 81% of voters — the third of three racial justice ballot measures) mandates the City to create and annually report a measure of what it actually costs to live in New York City, and the measure defines the threshold as 'the resources necessary for individuals and families to be independent of public and private assistance.' The report itself states no numeric reduction target. Independent cross-check: FCNY/United Way/Center for Women's Welfare Self-Sufficiency Standard, 46% of working-age households below its threshold (2024 incomes, 2026 costs). Secondary equity benchmark: MOERJ's Preliminary Citywide Racial Equity Plan goal of closing racial TCOL gaps (Hispanic 77.6%, Black 65.6%, Asian/Pacific Islander 63.3%, White 43.7%).",
                "context": "The Bronx: 75.1% below the TCOL threshold — highest of the five boroughs and 13.3 points above citywide; 86.8% of persons in families with children; 15.6% below half the threshold (highest deep-hardship rate in the city); average resource gap $39,692. The report notes the Bronx has the lowest costs of any borough but also the least resources.\n\nCitywide for comparison: 61.8% of New Yorkers (5.04M of 8.16M residents) below the TCOL threshold citywide, measured AFTER government supports; 67% before supports, i.e. transfers close ~5 percentage points. Universe is persons in families, all ages. A further 3.58M New Yorkers are above the federal poverty line but still below the TCOL threshold (the 'missing middle'), and 13% of residents (~1M people) are below HALF the threshold. Families with children face a median cost of $159,197/yr against median resources of $124,007 (78% of cost), an average gap of $35,190 — of which market resources supply $95,277 and government transfers $28,730. Citywide, persons in families below the threshold have an average annual resource gap of $39,603 (median costs $122,919 vs median resources $78,993). 73% of NYC children (1.2M) live in families below the threshold, rising to 86.8% in the Bronx. The highest rate of any group: 92% of people in families with a self-care disability (working-age adults under 65), with an average gap of $76,178; the broader any-disability rates are 80.3% (under 65) and 68.1% (65+).\n\nA purpose-built official city measure rather than a federal proxy, mandated by Charter Amendment 3 (Nov 2022) and published in its inaugural edition by MOERJ with the Mayor's Office for Economic Opportunity and the Urban Institute — report dated March 2026, released April 6, 2026 as part of the Mamdani administration's first-100-days package. VINTAGE CAVEAT (important): all estimates come from the Urban Institute's ATTIS model applied to 2018 American Community Survey data projected to 2022, so this is a 2022-vintage estimate published in 2026, with a population base of 8.16M residents. The measure adapts the Urban Institute's True Cost of Economic Security framework and, unlike the NYCgov poverty measure, asks what it takes to be independent of assistance rather than whether a poverty threshold is met — the report is explicit that the two are complementary rather than directly comparable. The threshold includes housing, food, health care, child care, transportation, taxes, a savings target set at 10% of expenses, and other necessities. RECONCILIATION with the other 'True Cost of Living' series (per R11): FCNY/United Way's NYC True Cost of Living reports 46% of WORKING-AGE HOUSEHOLDS below threshold (2024 ACS 1-year PUMS, incomes adjusted to 2026 costs), and 50% in its 2023 report (1,298,212 households, 2,991,973 people) against 36% in 2021. The 16-point gap to 62% is not a discrepancy but four definitional differences: unit (persons vs households), universe (all residents vs working-age households, FCNY explicitly excluding adults 65+, group quarters, and adults with work-limiting disabilities — precisely MOERJ's highest-rate groups), threshold basket (savings target included vs Self-Sufficiency Standard with none), and vintage (2022 vs 2024/2026). Note the direction check: FCNY measures resources BEFORE any assistance and still lands lower than MOERJ's post-transfer figure, confirming transfers are not the driver of the gap. TREND: the FCNY series is the only one with a real time trend and it declined 2012-2019, rose sharply in 2021, and declined again by 2024 — still 'substantially higher than pre-pandemic levels, despite declining from the 2021 peak.' FCNY attributes the decline to composition rather than easing affordability: fewer no-worker households as the labour market recovered, and a 1.2pp fall in the share of households with children (who face higher thresholds). Critically, inadequacy among households with one full-time year-round worker ROSE from 40% (2021) to 44% (2024), and rose among all working households — full-time work is increasingly insufficient. The scale dwarfs a small city like Petaluma: a majority of an 8.2M-person city cannot cover basic needs while working, and the Hispanic-white gap (77.6% vs 43.7%) is itself wider than the entire gap between Petaluma's poverty rate and the US average. Policy levers: MOERJ's Preliminary Citywide Racial Equity Plan (45 agencies, 200+ goals, 800+ strategies) and the Council's '30 For Our City' minimum-wage bill.",
                "source": "NYC MOERJ, 'NYC True Cost of Living Measure: Inaugural Report' (Tables 9-10)",
                "sourceUrl": "https://www.nyc.gov/assets/equity/downloads/pdf/2026%20NYC%20TCOL%20Measure_4%206%2026.pdf",
                "screenshot": null,
                "confidence": "high",
