                "comparisonNote": "Petaluma's nitrogen and phosphorus story is groundwater nitrate near agricultural land and septic systems (most wells below the 10 mg/L drinking-water standard, with 4-7 mg/L hotspots near agricultural boundaries, scored level 20). NYC's is an engineered wastewater-and-CSO story roughly three orders of magnitude larger: approximately 18 billion gallons a year of combined sewage through 398 permitted outfalls, and 14 WRRFs discharging some 132,200 lb/day of total nitrogen (~21,900 t N/year) into an estuarine system. There is essentially no diffuse agricultural-runoff analog in NYC's version of this dimension, and no groundwater-nitrate analog either, since the city draws its water from upstate reservoirs rather than local wells. The structural difference cuts both ways: NYC's loading is concentrated at monitored point sources, which is why it has been cut by 57-68% under two enforceable nutrient targets that have both been met — a lever Petaluma's diffuse problem does not offer — while NYC's untreated wet-weather discharge has no Petaluma counterpart at all and is on a fix timeline running to 2053.",
                "boroughs": null,
