                "context": "The Bronx: 6.51 µg/m³ (author-computed unweighted mean of the Bronx's 12 community districts, 2024)\n\nCitywide for comparison: 6.26 ug/m3 citywide annual average PM2.5 (2024) — DOHMH's OWN PUBLISHED citywide figure, which supersedes this dossier's original author-computed unweighted mean across the 59 community districts. 25% above the WHO 2021 guideline of 5 ug/m3. No NYC county is in PM2.5 nonattainment; monitor design values run 7.7-8.3 against the 9.0 NAAQS. Trend since 2009 is strongly favourable: PM2.5 -36%, NO2 -41%, black carbon -56%, SO2 -98%.\n\nPARTIALLY-APPLIED REVIEW: the adversarial reviewer completed its review but was cut off before writing a full corrected dossier. The headline value, severity level and rationale here are the reviewer's; the remaining 17 findings (1 critical, 8 major, 8 minor) are recorded in research/nyc/reviews/air-pollution.review.json and have NOT all been applied to the fields below. Known critical correction not yet propagated everywhere: the No. 4 heating-oil phase-out deadline of January 1 2030 was SUPERSEDED by Local Law 32 of 2023. Treat sub-indicators and policy anchors in this dimension as unverified pending re-application.\n\nNYC's PM2.5 story is fundamentally about intra-city gradient, not a single citywide number: the ~2x range from Rockaway (Queens, 5.3 µg/m³) to Midtown (Manhattan, 10.5 µg/m³) reflects traffic density, building-heating combustion, and street-canyon effects, not a uniform exposure. NYCCAS is a ~100-site seasonal monitoring network run by DOHMH and Queens College (CUNY) covering all 59 community districts since 2009 — one of the most granular urban air-monitoring systems in the US. Local Law 43 (2010) and the NYC Clean Heat program are the biggest documented policy win, having eliminated No. 6 heating oil citywide by 2015 and cut PM2.5 emissions from heating by an estimated 65%. Wildfire smoke (see subIndicators) is now a major confounder that DOHMH itself flags as disrupting the underlying downward trend. Congestion pricing (since Jan 2025) is independently measured to be reducing PM2.5 in and near the Manhattan CBD.",
