                "comparisonNote": "Counter-intuitively, NYC's production-based per-capita footprint (6.2 tCO2e/person, 2023, MOCEJ-published; roughly 5.7 derived for 2024) is LOWER than Petaluma's 7.25 tCO2e/person (2024), driven by density, transit ridership and small, efficient housing — even though NYC's absolute emissions of 47.9 MtCO2e (2024) are roughly 110x Petaluma's ~0.435 MtCO2e on about 141x the population. Note that the two portraits score this dimension against different targets: Petaluma's local level is set against the DEAL 1.6-2.0 tCO2e/person benchmark, whereas NYC's local level is set against NYC's own statutory 40%-below-2005-by-2030 target, which is why the wedges differ more than the underlying per-capita figures do. NYC's consumption-based footprint (11 tCO2e/person, 2019) is where the comparison genuinely flips: it captures the embodied carbon in everything the city imports and consumes, which a small-city inventory like Petaluma's does not attempt to measure at all. It is this global/consumption lens — not the production lens — where NYC's status as a global financial and consumption centre actually shows up.",
