                "target": "Reduce CSO volume by 1.67 billion gallons per year by December 2040 for the average rainfall year of 2008, with $3.5B in green infrastructure encumbered by December 2040 and expended by December 2045 (2023 Citywide Green Infrastructure Modification, DEC Case No. CO2-20230228-38, effective 29 June 2023, modifying the 2005 CSO Order CO2-20000107-8). Interim milestones: 668 MGY / $1.4B encumbered by December 2025; 700-800 MGY / $1.8B by December 2030; 850-1,220 MGY / $2.8B by December 2035.",
                "context": "⚠ Citywide figure shown — not specific to Brooklyn. No borough-level CSO volume is published. PROXIES, each measuring a different quantity than the headline: (1) the four Brooklyn WRRFs discharged CY2025 means of ~2,617 lb/day (26th Ward, NY0026212), ~8,108 (Coney Island, NY0026182), ~26,342 (Newtown Creek, NY0026204), ~14,633 (Owls Head, NY0026166) and ~4,801 (Red Hook, NY0027073) total nitrogen — nitrogen loads, not CSO volumes, and assigned by plant location: 26th Ward and Coney Island discharge to Jamaica Bay, which borders Queens as well, and Newtown Creek WRRF discharges to the East River. (2) The Gowanus Canal receives an estimated 659 million gallons/year of CSO under its approved LTCP baseline, which is modelled for the 2008 average/typical rainfall year. (3) The 26th Ward carbon-addition facility, completed in February 2012 at ~$2M, cut >3,000 lb/day of nitrogen, then about 10% of total Jamaica Bay loading. Note also that 26th Ward is in Significant/Category I Noncompliance on its permit, with 9 quarters of SNC and copper, TSS, silver and whole-effluent-toxicity exceedances — a chemical-pollution issue rather than a nutrient one, flagged for the chemical pollution dimension.\n\nThis is a wastewater-and-CSO story, not an agricultural-runoff story — the sharp structural contrast with Petaluma, where the same dimension is scored on groundwater nitrate near farms and septic systems. NYC's 14 water resource recovery facilities process about 1.3 billion gallons a day, and 12 of those 14 sewersheds are combined-sewered, containing 398-418 permitted CSO outfalls (DEC's current figure is 398; DEC's 2021 CSO Annual Report says 400 across the 12 combined sewersheds; the 2023 consent order itself recites 'approximately 418'; DEP's website's figure of 700 is a broader count of combined-sewer outfalls, not a superseded CSO count). Nitrogen and pathogen loading are therefore concentrated at engineered point sources tied to rain events, not diffuse land-based leaching. The nitrogen side of this dimension has genuinely succeeded: about $1.2B was spent between 1999 and 2022 upgrading 8 plants for biological nitrogen removal, and the Long Island Sound and Jamaica Bay nutrient targets are both met. The CSO side has not: ~$5.3B was spent on CSO and green infrastructure between 1995 and 2024 with ~$7.4B more programmed for 2025-2050, yet the committed reduction is only 1.67 BGY and the major grey projects run past mid-century. Caveat on the headline: DEP's 16 December 2025 Flushing Bay presentation shows an 'Untreated CSO Volume Reduction' graphic labelled 5.6 BGY (Baseline CSO & GI Program, $6.2B) and 3.5 BGY (LTCP CSO Program, $8.7B) which could not be reconciled with the 18 BGY figure from the extracted slide text; whether 18 BGY is a pre-programme baseline or a current modelled volume is unresolved.",
                "source": "NYSDEC 'New York City CSO Program' and statewide CSO pages (citing NYC DEP data); 2023 Citywide Green Infrastructure Order on Consent (DEC Case No. CO2-20230228-38); NYC DEP 'State of the Sewers 2024'",
