                    "indicator": "NYC residents' embodied/consumption-based nitrogen footprint, benchmarked against an equal-per-capita share of the planetary nitrogen boundary; with the US consumer phosphorus footprint as the phosphorus counterpart",
                    "value": "No NYC-specific study exists. Best available anchors: US average per-capita nitrogen footprint 41 kg N/person/year, of which food production is ~30 kg (Leach et al. 2012, N-Calculator/N-Print). Baltimore City community nitrogen footprint 30 kg N/person/year, ~19,000 metric tons N total in 2016, 73% from food production — the closest published US municipal-scale analysis. US consumer phosphorus footprint 4.4 kg P/person/year, against a food-portion nitrogen footprint of 22.4 kg N/person/year, i.e. ~5.2 kg N released per kg P (Metson/Leach et al., Environ. Res. Lett. 2020). Applying the 30-41 kg N/person/year range to ~8.26M residents implies an order-of-magnitude footprint of roughly 250,000-340,000 metric tons N/year, and the 4.4 kg P figure implies roughly 36,000 metric tons P/year — both explicitly extrapolations from non-NYC per-capita figures, not measured NYC quantities.",
                    "context": "NYC's nitrogen and phosphorus footprint is transmitted globally through its food system: importing produce, grain, meat and dairy from farms using synthetic nitrogen fertiliser manufactured from fossil gas and phosphate fertiliser from mined rock, a finite resource heavily concentrated in Morocco/Western Sahara. Roughly 19 billion pounds of food pass through the city each year, and household food consumption accounts for about 20% of the city's consumption-based greenhouse gas footprint — the same import dependence that drives the nitrogen footprint. The Baltimore City analysis (Environ. Res. Lett. 2020) shows this accounting is feasible at municipal scale and that the dominant term is food production (73%), with energy and transport a combined 15%; it also found substantial variation across census block groups driven by income and development, which is the equity handle for NYC. On the phosphorus side, the return loop is broken: DEP produces approximately 1,300 wet tons of dewatered biosolids a day (~475,000 wet tons/year) across its 14 WRRFs, was landfilling 100% as of 2010, and currently beneficially uses roughly 31% against a stated goal of diverting 100% from landfill by 2030. Every ton landfilled rather than land-applied is phosphorus not returned to agricultural soil, against a finite global phosphate-rock reserve. No NYC phosphorus-recovery programme (struvite precipitation or equivalent) was identified in any source consulted, so the recovery framing is real in principle but unquantified and unimplemented here.",
                    "source": "Planetary Health Check / Richardson et al. 2023 (boundary values); Leach et al. 2012 N-Calculator (US per-capita N footprint); Deemer/Wang et al., 'A community nitrogen footprint analysis of Baltimore City, Maryland', Environ. Res. Lett. 15 075007 (2020); Metson/Leach et al., 'The U.S. consumer phosphorus footprint', Environ. Res. Lett. (2020); NEBRA reporting on NYC DEP biosolids (Mar 2024)",
                    "sourceUrl": "https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/ab76dc"
