                "levelRationale": "Anchored on the headline: NYC discharges approximately 18 billion gallons a year of untreated combined sewage through 398 permitted CSO outfalls into a eutrophication- and pathogen-sensitive estuary, and the binding abatement commitment is only 1.67 billion gallons a year — roughly 9% of that load — on a deadline that has already slid from 2030 to December 2040 with a December 2045 backstop. Pushed up by three well-sourced shortfalls named individually: the Flushing Bay CSO control tunnel is now scheduled for construction completion in Q4 2053 at $3.42B (from $683M and 2035 in the approved LTCP); green/managed acreage stands at 2,062 acres against the ~8,000 acres implied by the 10%-of-impervious 2030 goal, and DEP missed the 1.5%-by-2015 and 4%-by-2020 application-rate milestones outright; and citywide WRRF effluent nitrogen is ~132,200 lb/day (~21,900 t N/yr, ~2.6 kg N/person/yr), roughly half of per-capita excreted nitrogen, meaning the city removes only about half the nitrogen it collects. Pulled down — but only as a modifier, per R12, not as the anchor — by three genuinely met nutrient targets: the Long Island Sound point-source load is 68% below the TMDL baseline against a 60% requirement (18,909 lb/day, 2025); Jamaica Bay WRRF nitrogen is ~17,100 lb/day against ~40,000 in early 2010, a ~57% cut against a >50% target; and harbor-wide summer dissolved oxygen has exceeded the NYS 5.0 mg/L standard every summer since the 1990s. Also pulled down modestly by DEP certifying its December 2025 consent-order milestone (668 MGY of CSO reduction, $1.4B encumbered) on time, on 30 December 2025. Not scored lower because meeting one's own consent order is not the same as living within planetary means: nitrogen is among the most-transgressed planetary boundaries (~190 Tg N/yr against a 62 Tg boundary), and a chronic multi-billion-gallon untreated discharge with a 2053 fix date is not inside the safe space.",
