                    "level": "NaN",
                    "indicator": "NYC's external mobility footprint and its transferability as a low-car megacity model -- qualitative pending quantification of (a) rolling-stock and electric-bus supply-chain sourcing, (b) aviation emissions excluded from the city inventory boundary, and (c) the transport-mode-share transferability argument",
                    "value": "NOT QUANTIFIED. Three transmission channels are identified but none has a defensible NYC-specific number in this pass. (1) TRANSFERABILITY: NYC is the one large US city where a large majority of workers do not use a car to get to work (25.1% used a car, truck or van at all; 48.7% used public transit; 9.6% walked), which is the empirical basis on which dense transit-oriented development is argued globally to be compatible with a 1.5C per-capita transport budget. Quantifying NYC's per-capita transport emissions against a downscaled global transport budget would turn this into a real global indicator; that calculation has not been done here. (2) SUPPLY CHAIN: the embodied emissions and labour conditions in MTA rolling-stock procurement and electric-bus/battery sourcing are a genuine external footprint, but no NYC-specific quantification was located and this channel was not researched in this pass. (3) AVIATION: JFK, LaGuardia and Newark aviation emissions are widely reported to fall outside NYC's official GPC-protocol greenhouse-gas inventory boundary, which would make a substantial share of the mobility emissions NYC induces invisible in its own accounting. The primary source for this (Citizens Budget Commission) returned HTTP 403 on two separate fetch attempts, including during adversarial review, and the claim is therefore NOT asserted -- it remains a dataGap for MOCEJ and the Port Authority of NY & NJ to resolve. GLOBAL-EQUITY COUNTERPOINT (context, not an NYC-attributable metric): roughly 900 million to 1 billion rural people worldwide lack access to an all-season road (World Bank Rural Access Index / SDG indicator 9.1.1), a stark contrast with NYC's 24/7 rail system, but not something NYC policy moves.",
                    "context": "NYC's below-25%-car mode share is precisely the outcome climate and transit advocates worldwide point to as proof that dense transit-oriented cities work, and the city exports that model through comparative research and city networks (Transit Costs Project, ITDP, C40). The cost subIndicator below is the cautionary half of that story -- at $4B+ per mile NYC cannot build enough subway to close its own accessibility gaps -- but per R2 it describes NYC's governance, not NYC's effect on others, and so cannot carry this lens. The honest position is that mobility's global lens is currently unquantified, and that the fastest route to quantifying it is either the aviation-boundary question (MOCEJ / Port Authority) or a rolling-stock procurement footprint (MTA Construction & Development).",
                    "source": "See subIndicators and dataGaps; no single source carries this lens.",
                    "sourceUrl": "https://www.mta.info/press-release/mta-construction-development-spotlights-action-taken-build-better-faster-and-cheaper"
