                    "indicator": "NYC Household Consumption-Based Emissions Inventory (CBEI) — lifecycle/scope-3 emissions from NYC residents' household consumption of food, goods, housing, services and private transport, wherever in the world those emissions physically occur",
                    "value": "92 million MTCO2e for NYC household consumption in 2019 across 3,211,033 households — about 29 MTCO2e per household, or about 11 MTCO2e per person. SCOPE: household consumption by NYC residents only; the CBEI excludes business and tourism consumption, non-residential building energy, and government/institutional consumption. Category breakdown per household: Food 7.5 t (25% of the total; eating out 1.7 t and animal products 1.8 t, each about 23% of food emissions), Housing 7.0 t (natural gas 3.7 t, about 53% and the largest single component), Services 5.9 t (healthcare 3.4 t, 57%), Goods 4.2 t (furnishings and appliances 1.6 t, 38%), Private transportation 4.1 t (air travel alone 1.4 t, 34% of the transport share). Personal air travel citywide therefore totals roughly 4.5 million MTCO2e/year (DERIVED: 1.4 t/household x 3.21 million households) — and this captures only NYC residents' personal flights, not the far larger throughput of non-resident passengers transiting JFK/LaGuardia/Newark, which is not attributable to the city under this method. Emissions vary roughly 3-fold between census tracts: 58 tCO2e/household in the highest-emitting tract against 18 in the lowest, with the citywide average at 29.",
                    "context": "This is the single most important entry in NYC's ecological portrait: it is the number that shows the small-city framing fails for a global megacity. THE FINDING, stated so it does not depend on a ratio: NYC residents' household consumption alone drives 92 MtCO2e/yr — 11 tCO2e per person — and the great majority of those emissions occur outside the five boroughs, entirely invisible to the territorial inventory that Local Law 97 and the 40x30 target are tracked against. MOCEJ puts it plainly: 'the vast majority of goods and services consumed in New York City are produced elsewhere. This means that most emissions in the CBEI are scope 3.' C40 quantifies the same channel network-wide: 85% of the emissions associated with goods and services consumed in C40 cities are generated outside the city — 60% elsewhere in the same country and 25% abroad. THE 67% COMPARISON, WITH ITS CAVEAT: the CBEI's own conclusion is that 92 MtCO2e is 'about 67% more than in the territorial inventory' (which it states as 55 MtCO2e for 2019), and that 'a CBEI that is much larger than a territorial inventory indicates that New York City is a net exporter of emissions.' Quote that as the source's own framing, with three qualifications. First, the two accountings are not a partition of the same universe: the CBEI counts only resident household consumption while the territorial inventory counts all in-boundary activity including commercial, industrial and freight, so the comparison is directional evidence rather than an arithmetic difference. Second, MOCEJ's own integration paper is explicit that the two inventories 'are developed using very different methods that are not directly compatible', and combining them into an Integrated Inventory required an estimated overlap correction — roughly 17 million MTCO2e of residential energy use is common to both. They must not be summed or subtracted. Third, the 55 MtCO2e territorial figure is a superseded vintage: the current NYC Open Data series puts CY2019 Citywide-GPC at 52.69 MtCO2e, against which 92 Mt is 75% larger. The cleanest single sentence for citation: NYC residents' household consumption footprint of 11 tCO2e/person is roughly 1.8x the entire city's territorial footprint per resident (about 6.3 tCO2e/person in 2019), even though the household figure counts only households. TRANSMISSION MECHANISMS: trade (food, goods, construction materials), personal aviation, institutional procurement, and finance — NYC-headquartered banks underwriting fossil-fuel projects worldwide, a separate channel the consumption inventory does not cover and which remains unquantified here. POLICY LEVER: categorically different from Local Law 97 — consumer behaviour, supply-chain standards such as the Good Food Purchasing Program, and national/global production decarbonisation NYC cannot mandate. C40's modelling credits city governments and urban stakeholders with delivering 35% of the necessary reductions on their own, and finds that meeting Deadline 2020 production-based commitments alone addresses the ~25% of consumption emissions that overlaps the territorial inventory and closes 80% of the consumption gap; the remainder depends on the carbon intensity of production worldwide.",
                    "source": "NYC Household Consumption-Based Emissions Inventory (EcoDataLab with C40 Cities and MOCEJ, funded by American Express), 2019 base year, published February 2023; methodological relationship to the territorial inventory from MOCEJ, 'Integrating New York City's 2019 Citywide and Consumption Based Greenhouse Gas Emissions Inventories'; global transmission figures from C40, 'The Future of Urban Consumption in a 1.5C World' (2019)",
                    "sourceUrl": "https://www.nyc.gov/assets/climate/downloads/pdfs/NYC-Household-Consumption-GHG-Emissions-Inventory.pdf"
