                "value": "Approximately 18 billion gallons of combined sewage discharged citywide per year through 398 permitted CSO outfalls; approximately 60% of the city's sewer system is combined. This is NYSDEC's published modelled average-year estimate and carries no stated vintage; DEP's LTCP modelling is normalised to the 'average rainfall year of 2008' and DEP publishes no measured citywide annual total.",
                "year": null,
                "target": "Reduce CSO volume by 1.67 billion gallons per year by December 2040 for the average rainfall year of 2008, with $3.5B in green infrastructure encumbered by December 2040 and expended by December 2045 (2023 Citywide Green Infrastructure Modification, DEC Case No. CO2-20230228-38, effective 29 June 2023, modifying the 2005 CSO Order CO2-20000107-8). Interim milestones: 668 MGY / $1.4B encumbered by December 2025; 700-800 MGY / $1.8B by December 2030; 850-1,220 MGY / $2.8B by December 2035.",
                "context": "⚠ Citywide figure shown — not specific to Manhattan. No borough-level CSO volume is published. PROXY, measuring a different quantity than the headline: Manhattan's two WRRFs discharged CY2025 means of ~19,067 lb/day (North River, NY0026247, to the Hudson) and ~14,542 lb/day (Wards Island, NY0026131, to the upper East River) total nitrogen. Assigned by plant location and materially misleading if read as Manhattan's load: Wards Island sits on Randall's Island and discharges to the upper East River, a reach shared with Queens and the Bronx and tributary to Long Island Sound. Historical context: Save the Sound reported in March 2013 that the Wards Island nitrogen upgrade cut discharge from 33,000 to 17,000 lb/day; the CY2025 figure is lower still.\n\nThis is a wastewater-and-CSO story, not an agricultural-runoff story — the sharp structural contrast with Petaluma, where the same dimension is scored on groundwater nitrate near farms and septic systems. NYC's 14 water resource recovery facilities process about 1.3 billion gallons a day, and 12 of those 14 sewersheds are combined-sewered, containing 398-418 permitted CSO outfalls (DEC's current figure is 398; DEC's 2021 CSO Annual Report says 400 across the 12 combined sewersheds; the 2023 consent order itself recites 'approximately 418'; DEP's website's figure of 700 is a broader count of combined-sewer outfalls, not a superseded CSO count). Nitrogen and pathogen loading are therefore concentrated at engineered point sources tied to rain events, not diffuse land-based leaching. The nitrogen side of this dimension has genuinely succeeded: about $1.2B was spent between 1999 and 2022 upgrading 8 plants for biological nitrogen removal, and the Long Island Sound and Jamaica Bay nutrient targets are both met. The CSO side has not: ~$5.3B was spent on CSO and green infrastructure between 1995 and 2024 with ~$7.4B more programmed for 2025-2050, yet the committed reduction is only 1.67 BGY and the major grey projects run past mid-century. Caveat on the headline: DEP's 16 December 2025 Flushing Bay presentation shows an 'Untreated CSO Volume Reduction' graphic labelled 5.6 BGY (Baseline CSO & GI Program, $6.2B) and 3.5 BGY (LTCP CSO Program, $8.7B) which could not be reconciled with the 18 BGY figure from the extracted slide text; whether 18 BGY is a pre-programme baseline or a current modelled volume is unresolved.",
