                "levelRationale": "The headline alone supports something close to 25: DOHMH's published citywide annual mean of 6.26 µg/m³ (2024) is 25% above the WHO 2021 guideline of 5 µg/m³, no NYC county is designated nonattainment for PM2.5, monitor design values run 7.7-8.3 against a 9.0 NAAQS, and the trend is strongly favourable (PM2.5 −36%, NO2 −41%, black carbon −56%, SO2 −98% since 2009). Per R1 the level must then be adjusted for well-sourced shortfalls inside the same dimension, and there are four material ones. (1) Ozone: all five boroughs sit in 'Serious' 2015-ozone-NAAQS nonattainment with an August 3, 2027 attainment deadline, and NYCCAS shows no downward ozone trend at all (24.3 ppb in 2009 to 32.3 ppb in 2024) with DOHMH expecting it to worsen with warming — a dimension cannot be 'mild' when the region formally fails a health standard and the failing pollutant is flat-to-rising. (2) Mortality and morbidity burden: DOHMH attributes ~2,000 excess deaths/yr (1 in 25 deaths citywide) to long-term PM2.5, with a modelled 1,798 deaths/yr age 30+ and 1,125 pediatric asthma ED visits/yr in the 2017-2019 vintage. (3) Distributional failure: child asthma ED visits range from 441.8 per 10,000 in Brownsville to 20.6 in Tottenville–Great Kills (2023), roughly 21x, and Manhattan's PM2.5 (7.71) is 30% above Queens's (5.95) with Midtown at 10.5 — the citywide mean conceals an exposure gradient the dimension exists to show. (4) Episodic overshoot: two severe wildfire-smoke events in three years (AQI 465 in June 2023, AQI 289 across five days in July 2026 alongside a heat emergency), and the 2023 citywide regulatory-monitor annual average of 10.8 µg/m³ exceeded the federal standard outright. On the other side, R5 forbids manufacturing a crisis: this is a city with a genuine multi-decade improvement record, the most granular urban monitoring network in the US, and a documented policy win in the residual-oil phase-out. 100 would be doom inflation and 25 launders the ozone failure and the mortality burden. 50 — 'needs attention' — is the honest reading. The global lens stays at 50: the outbound channels are now quantified (port share of county NOx up to 17.5%, the only tri-state monitor above the 9.0 NAAQS sitting inside the port complex at 9.4 µg/m³, and NY-source contributions above 14 ppb on Connecticut's worst ozone days), so R2's requirement for quantification is met and a score rather than null is justified.",
