                "value": "47.94 MtCO2e citywide in 2024 (exact: 47,944,551 tCO2e) — down 25.2% from the 2005 baseline of 64.14 MtCO2e (64,139,721 tCO2e) and down 4.9% from 2023's 50.43 MtCO2e (50,433,720 tCO2e), the largest single-year reduction in the 20-year series. Per capita: MOCEJ's most recently published figure is 6.2 tCO2e/person (2023), which MOCEJ notes is 'over 50% lower than the 2020 U.S. average of 13 tCO2e per capita'; a CY2024 per-capita figure has not yet been published, and 47.94 Mt over the Census Bureau's Vintage-2024 July-2024 city population of 8,478,000 implies roughly 5.7 tCO2e/person (DERIVED here, not an official MOCEJ figure). Exact CY2024 sector shares: Stationary Energy (buildings) 69.5%, Transportation 26.3%, Waste 4.2%. (CY2023 for comparison: 71.2% / 24.9% / 3.9%; MOCEJ's own rounded slide labels for 2023 were 72%/25%/4%, which sum to 101%.)",
                "year": 2024,
                "target": "Local Law 22 of 2008 mandates the Citywide-GPC inventory that tracks Local Law 97/PlaNYC goals: 40% reduction below 2005 levels by 2030 (MOCEJ slide: 'Carbon Reduction Targets: Citywide: 40% by 2030'), and carbon neutrality by 2050. NY State's CLCPA sets a parallel but distinct target (40% below 1990 by 2030, 85% by 2050, 70% renewable electricity by 2030), tracked via a separate 'Citywide-CLCPA' inventory that additionally counts fugitive energy-related emissions and uses a 20-yr GWP to highlight methane's near-term warming — carried as a subIndicator below, not averaged into the headline.",
                "context": "⚠ Citywide figure shown — not specific to Queens. Not published; see Bronx note. Consumption-based CBEI proxy (different basis): Queens = 12 tCO2e/person (2019).\n\nAt 47.9 MtCO2e total, NYC's absolute footprint is roughly 110x Petaluma's (~0.435 MtCO2e) on about 141x the population, and its production-based per-capita figure of 6.2 tCO2e (2023, MOCEJ-published) is genuinely LOWER than Petaluma's 7.25 tCO2e/person (2024) — a real, counter-intuitive result driven by density, transit ridership, and small, efficient housing units. The policy lever is different in kind from a small city's: Local Law 97 imposes binding, penalty-backed emissions caps on nearly 50,000 covered properties, holding about 60% of the city's building floor area and about 50% of building-sector emissions (roughly 35% of citywide emissions), enforced property-by-property rather than through a single utility or fleet decision. The major recent complication is the April 2021 closure of the Indian Point nuclear plant, which MOCEJ's own analysis shows increased in-city fossil generation and grid carbon intensity (289.6 to 369.9 kg CO2/MWh, 2019-2023; renewables share of NYC's grid 36% to 14%; share of grid served by in-city plants 47% to 59%) and concentrated the resulting pollution increase in disadvantaged waterfront neighborhoods. MOCEJ also reports NYC as the second-largest total emitter among C40 cities, with its production-based per-capita emissions ranking 'just above average' among C40 members.",
                "source": "NYC Open Data, 'NYC Greenhouse Gas Emissions Inventory' (dataset wq7q-htne, MOCEJ, updated 2025-12-16, includes CY2024); per-capita and reduction figures from MOCEJ, 'NYC 2023 Greenhouse Gas Inventory Methodology'; grid, sector and trajectory analysis from MOCEJ's 'NYC Greenhouse Gas Inventory' presentation to the Climate Knowledge Exchange, January 13, 2025",
                "sourceUrl": "https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Environment/NYC-Greenhouse-Gas-Emissions-Inventory/wq7q-htne",
