                "context": "⚠ Citywide figure shown — not specific to Queens. No borough-level CSO volume is published. PROXIES, each measuring a different quantity than the headline: (1) Queens WRRFs discharged CY2025 means of ~11,642 lb/day (Bowery Bay, NY0026158), ~4,892 (Tallman Island, NY0026239), ~5,158 (Jamaica, NY0026115) and ~1,242 (Rockaway, NY0026221) total nitrogen. Bowery Bay and Tallman Island are East River/Long Island Sound dischargers; Jamaica and Rockaway discharge to Jamaica Bay, shared with Brooklyn. (2) Capital status: the approved 2017 Flushing Bay LTCP requires a 25 million gallon CSO storage tunnel capturing outfalls BB-006 and BB-008; DEP's proposed December 2025 modification would instead deliver 6.5 MG of in-line storage (construction complete Q2 2037) plus a 40 MG tunnel (construction complete Q4 2053) at a combined $3.42B, versus $683M and 2035 in the 2016 LTCP. The modification request was submitted to DEC on 31 December 2025 and was still under DEC review as of the Q1 2026 quarterly report. (3) Flushing Bay and Flushing Creek together account for 323.4 green/managed acres and 162.4 MGY of certified CSO reduction, the largest outside Jamaica Bay.\n\nThis is a wastewater-and-CSO story, not an agricultural-runoff story — the sharp structural contrast with Petaluma, where the same dimension is scored on groundwater nitrate near farms and septic systems. NYC's 14 water resource recovery facilities process about 1.3 billion gallons a day, and 12 of those 14 sewersheds are combined-sewered, containing 398-418 permitted CSO outfalls (DEC's current figure is 398; DEC's 2021 CSO Annual Report says 400 across the 12 combined sewersheds; the 2023 consent order itself recites 'approximately 418'; DEP's website's figure of 700 is a broader count of combined-sewer outfalls, not a superseded CSO count). Nitrogen and pathogen loading are therefore concentrated at engineered point sources tied to rain events, not diffuse land-based leaching. The nitrogen side of this dimension has genuinely succeeded: about $1.2B was spent between 1999 and 2022 upgrading 8 plants for biological nitrogen removal, and the Long Island Sound and Jamaica Bay nutrient targets are both met. The CSO side has not: ~$5.3B was spent on CSO and green infrastructure between 1995 and 2024 with ~$7.4B more programmed for 2025-2050, yet the committed reduction is only 1.67 BGY and the major grey projects run past mid-century. Caveat on the headline: DEP's 16 December 2025 Flushing Bay presentation shows an 'Untreated CSO Volume Reduction' graphic labelled 5.6 BGY (Baseline CSO & GI Program, $6.2B) and 3.5 BGY (LTCP CSO Program, $8.7B) which could not be reconciled with the 18 BGY figure from the extracted slide text; whether 18 BGY is a pre-programme baseline or a current modelled volume is unresolved.",
