                "year": null,
                "target": "Reduce CSO volume by 1.67 billion gallons per year by December 2040 for the average rainfall year of 2008, with $3.5B in green infrastructure encumbered by December 2040 and expended by December 2045 (2023 Citywide Green Infrastructure Modification, DEC Case No. CO2-20230228-38, effective 29 June 2023, modifying the 2005 CSO Order CO2-20000107-8). Interim milestones: 668 MGY / $1.4B encumbered by December 2025; 700-800 MGY / $1.8B by December 2030; 850-1,220 MGY / $2.8B by December 2035.",
                "context": "⚠ Citywide figure shown — not specific to Staten Island. No borough-level CSO volume is published, and there is no Staten Island waterbody LTCP among the 10 waterbody plans plus 1 citywide plan. IMPORTANT CORRECTION TO A COMMON CLAIM: Staten Island is NOT largely separately sewered — it is split roughly in half. The Oakwood Beach WRRF sewershed (NY0026174; 10,779-acre drainage area, ~245,000 people, southern and eastern Staten Island) is not a combined system and has no CSO outfalls, per DEC's 2021 CSO Annual Report. The Port Richmond WRRF sewershed (NY0026107; 9,665-acre drainage area, ~200,000 people, north shore) treats combined sewage and does have CSO outfalls. By drainage acreage that is about 47% combined / 53% separate; by served population about 45% / 55%. The Bluebelt program — ~10,000 acres across 16 watersheds using natural drainage corridors — sits within the already-separately-sewered southern and eastern part of the island, so it explains stormwater management there rather than explaining an absence of CSOs borough-wide. PROXY, measuring a different quantity than the headline: the two Staten Island WRRFs discharged CY2025 means of ~9,017 lb/day (Oakwood Beach) and ~3,550 lb/day (Port Richmond) total nitrogen.\n\nThis is a wastewater-and-CSO story, not an agricultural-runoff story — the sharp structural contrast with Petaluma, where the same dimension is scored on groundwater nitrate near farms and septic systems. NYC's 14 water resource recovery facilities process about 1.3 billion gallons a day, and 12 of those 14 sewersheds are combined-sewered, containing 398-418 permitted CSO outfalls (DEC's current figure is 398; DEC's 2021 CSO Annual Report says 400 across the 12 combined sewersheds; the 2023 consent order itself recites 'approximately 418'; DEP's website's figure of 700 is a broader count of combined-sewer outfalls, not a superseded CSO count). Nitrogen and pathogen loading are therefore concentrated at engineered point sources tied to rain events, not diffuse land-based leaching. The nitrogen side of this dimension has genuinely succeeded: about $1.2B was spent between 1999 and 2022 upgrading 8 plants for biological nitrogen removal, and the Long Island Sound and Jamaica Bay nutrient targets are both met. The CSO side has not: ~$5.3B was spent on CSO and green infrastructure between 1995 and 2024 with ~$7.4B more programmed for 2025-2050, yet the committed reduction is only 1.67 BGY and the major grey projects run past mid-century. Caveat on the headline: DEP's 16 December 2025 Flushing Bay presentation shows an 'Untreated CSO Volume Reduction' graphic labelled 5.6 BGY (Baseline CSO & GI Program, $6.2B) and 3.5 BGY (LTCP CSO Program, $8.7B) which could not be reconciled with the 18 BGY figure from the extracted slide text; whether 18 BGY is a pre-programme baseline or a current modelled volume is unresolved.",
                "source": "NYSDEC 'New York City CSO Program' and statewide CSO pages (citing NYC DEP data); 2023 Citywide Green Infrastructure Order on Consent (DEC Case No. CO2-20230228-38); NYC DEP 'State of the Sewers 2024'",
